#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084147} :activity/id #uuid "4631a63f-ad43-556f-95cf-d62364c255c3" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050531 :topic/id #uuid "509b8ea0-3cc6-5f94-8f44-a3e6445a83e5" :topic/math-academy-id 798} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084147 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084390 :tutorial/content "A curve or function is defined parametrically if $x$ and $y$ are given in terms of a third parameter $t.$ This can often give rise to some interesting shapes. \n\nFor example, we can define a curve as \n\n$$\nx=t^3-3t, \\qquad y=3t^2-9, \\qquad -\\infty < t <\\infty.\n$$\n\nIf we plot the curve, it looks as follows:\n\n![](images/b3/b3d085ad763c368d7fafaca878c364fc13a0c83ca73b2fd17a2f349cb402a16e.png)\n\nIf we're given a parametric curve, how do we calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}?$ The answer is to use the chain rule. \n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t} \\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}t}{\\mathrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange this formula into a nicer version, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{dy}{dt}\\cdot \\frac{dt}{dx} \\\\  & =\\frac{dy}{dt}\\cdot \\frac{1}{dx/dt} \\\\  & =\\frac{dy/dt}{dx/dt} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use the above formula to calculate $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ for the given parametric curve\n\n$$\nx=t^3-3t, \\qquad y=3t^2-9.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t)=3t^2-3, \\qquad y'(t)=6t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6t}{3{t}^{2}-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2t}{{t}^{2}-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the curve is defined in terms of the parameter $t,$ the derivative is also defined in terms of $t.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "94a5a3d6-2fcd-522e-934f-f2bf8a8569c4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1068 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "fbbf0416-db87-510c-8e34-a3647e51dda7" :step/math-academy-id 644784 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084148}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084148 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083240 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083054 :question/id #uuid "15c029df-b002-51cc-87db-a94d40a02771" :question/math-academy-id "e-595"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8922ab07-5ac7-5f85-bfee-c30de7f4024a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051084}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522922} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083909} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083911} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290260} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321110} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321111} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321112} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321113} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321114} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321115} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335953} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335963} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368467} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401840} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403272} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403279} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522901} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522908} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522915} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522916} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522919} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522920} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541058}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Parametric Curve"} :step/id #uuid "4f8fcd3c-4b1b-56da-86a6-82a4b1a8cdce" :step/math-academy-id 644785 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084152}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084152 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083243 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083055 :question/id #uuid "148e5378-9177-59ba-9b1e-3633c940babd" :question/math-academy-id "e-596"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0bda136-40a9-576a-b85c-4d4ebefaeb27